# The pizzas the shop sells, and what is in them.
#
# This is *our* knowledge: it says what the kitchen puts on a pizza and what
# that means for a guest who does not eat meat, fish, milk or egg. The Pizza
# API (GET /pizza) knows two things about a pizza -- its id and its name; every
# other fact in this file is one the service does not have, which is the whole
# reason for the graph. `pz:apiId` is the join.
#
# The dietary flags on a pizza are *asserted* here so that a question answerer
# can read one triple instead of reasoning, and they are *derived* again from
# the toppings by `check_facts.py`. When the two disagree, one of them is wrong
# -- that is the repair loop, and it is a test, not a hope.
#
# Reading rule for toppings: a topping states only what it contains. No
# statement means "not present" (`pz:Basil` says nothing about milk because
# there is none in basil). The same closed reading is what makes
# `FILTER NOT EXISTS` in the queries mean what it looks like it means.

@prefix pz:   <http://example.org/pizza/> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

# =====================================================================
# the pizzas -- ids 1-22 of GET /pizza, in menu order
# =====================================================================

pz:Margherita a pz:Pizza ;
    pz:name         "Margherita" ;
    pz:apiId        1 ;
    pz:hasTopping   pz:Tomato, pz:Mozzarella, pz:Basil ;
    pz:containsMeat false ;
    pz:containsFish false ;
    pz:containsMilk true ;
    pz:containsEgg  false ;
    pz:vegetarian   true ;
    pz:vegan        false ;
    pz:description  "Tomato, mozzarella and fresh basil -- the classic." .

